{"timestamp":"2026-04-27T22:22:13.841Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"exec_command_end","call_id":"call_XRmrzqEryhOUxETZlVavxJVT","process_id":"98487","turn_id":"019dd108-f70c-75e2-8c81-d9a95744ebd0","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '1,260p' docs/superpowers/specs/2026-04-21-entire-why-design.md"],"cwd":"/Users/pfleidi/entire/cli/.worktrees/feat/entire-why","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,260p' docs/superpowers/specs/2026-04-21-entire-why-design.md","name":"2026-04-21-entire-why-design.md","path":"docs/superpowers/specs/2026-04-21-entire-why-design.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","stdout":"","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"# `entire why` Design\n\n## Goal\n\nIntroduce a prototype command named `entire why` that helps users understand why the current `HEAD` version of a file looks the way it does.\n\nThe command should feel like a blame-oriented history browser:\n\n- First view: a blame-derived full-file view of the current file\n- Second view: checkpoint detail for the selected blamed line's owning blame block when Entire metadata exists\n- Third view: session detail within that checkpoint\n- Optional no-arg interactive entry: a file browser rooted at the current working directory that lets the user choose a file before entering the why view\n\nFor commits that are not linked to Entire metadata, the command should still be useful by falling back to generic Git commit detail and a file-scoped diff.\n\n## Prototype Scope\n\nThe prototype is intentionally narrow.\n\nIncluded:\n\n- Command name: `entire why`\n- Input shape:\n  - `entire why <path>`\n  - `entire why -L <line> <path>`\n  - `entire why --lines <start-end> <path>`\n  - `entire why` in an interactive shell should open a file browser rooted at `pwd`\n- Explicit mode controls:\n  - `-i` / `--interactive` to force the TUI path\n  - `--no-pager` to disable pager use for static output\n- Local-only metadata resolution\n- `HEAD`-only blame view\n- Commit-first provenance model\n- Non-interactive text output\n- Interactive TUI when a real interactive shell is available\n- Checkpoint-first detail navigation\n- Summary text with fallback when no generated summary exists\n- Syntax highlighting in the TUI code pane\n\nExplicitly out of scope for the prototype:\n\n- Remote checkpoint fetching\n- Arbitrary revisions such as `entire why <rev> -- <path>`\n- Automatic summary generation during `entire why`\n- JSON output\n- Rename/copy-following flags such as `-M` and `-C`\n- Uncommitted working tree attribution\n\n## Product Decisions\n\n### Provenance Model\n\nThe first screen is commit-first, not checkpoint-first.\n\n`git blame` remains the source of truth for line provenance. Entire metadata enriches blamed commits by default when available, and that enrichment is part of the main value of `entire why`, not a secondary or hidden detail.\n\nThis is important because existing checkpoint/session storage is not sufficient to reconstruct exact current-line ownership without effectively reimplementing blame semantics.\n\n### History Coverage\n\nThe first screen should show the file itself in order from top to bottom, not a reduced list of blocks.\n\nEvery source line in the current `HEAD` version of the file should remain visible in both static and interactive modes. Entire metadata should enrich those lines, not replace the file view with a block browser.\n\nRows whose owning blame block has Entire metadata should be visually emphasized. Rows without Entire metadata should remain visible with blank or placeholder Entire fields.\n\n### Detail Hierarchy\n\nPressing `enter` or space on a selected blamed line should open a checkpoint-first detail screen when that line's owning blamed block has an `Entire-Checkpoint` trailer.\n\nIf no checkpoint exists for the blamed commit, detail falls back to generic Git commit information plus a file-scoped diff for the selected file.\n\n### Summary Policy\n\nThe first screen should show:\n\n- Saved generated summary text