wn\n- `k` = up\n- `l` = open / drill down / confirm\n\nThis means the documented controls remain accessibility-first while users familiar with vim-like navigation can still rely on the same directional vocabulary across views.\n\nThe TUI should include syntax highlighting in the code pane for the prototype.\n\nThe recommended approach is to use a pure-Go highlighter that emits ANSI-colored terminal text for direct rendering inside Bubble Tea viewports. The leading candidate is Chroma because it is pure Go, supports many languages, and can format to terminal color output. Markdown-focused renderers such as Glamour are less suitable as the primary engine because this screen is fundamentally a source-code browser, not a markdown document renderer.\n\nSyntax highlighting should be applied only to the code pane, not to the metadata gutter. The metadata side should remain styled via Lip Gloss so the two layers remain visually distinct.\n\nIn the interactive view, the selected line may also drive a compact metadata header showing richer fields such as date and summary, while the scrollable body stays focused on the file itself.\n\nThe interactive view should favor code readability over stuffing every Entire field into the per-line gutter. The settled layout direction is:\n\n- A compact left gutter for the file view with commit hash, author, checkpoint ID, and line number\n- A richer top metadata bar for the currently selected line, using labeled fields such as commit, author, date, checkpoint, agent, and summary\n- Detail screens that use clearly separated sections instead of one continuous text wall\n\nGeneral pager-style shortcuts should be supported in all TUI views:\n\n- `g` jumps to the top\n- `G` jumps to the bottom\n\nCheckpoint detail should use explicit focusable panes rather than treating the entire screen as one undifferentiated scroll surface. The current pane model is:\n\n- `Sessions`\n- `Generated Checkpoint Summary`\n- `File Diff`\n\n`tab` cycles forward through those panes when more than one is present. The focused pane should:\n\n- receive the active bordered-panel treatment\n- own `j` / `k` or arrow-key scrolling when it is a scrollable pane\n- expand to show materially more content than the unfocused panes\n\nUnfocused panes may render as previews rather than full sections. In particular:\n\n- the session list may show only the first few rows plus a “more sessions” hint\n- the generated summary may show only an initial preview when unfocused\n- the diff may show only an initial preview when unfocused\n\n### Interactive Entry Without a Path\n\nIf `entire why` is invoked with no path in an interactive shell, it should start in a lightweight file browser rooted at the current working directory.\n\nThis browser should:\n\n- Show all files and directories, including dotfiles\n- Sort directories before files\n- Allow directory traversal with `j`/`k`, arrows, `enter`, and `h` or `backspace`\n- Preserve browser directory and cursor position when the user opens a file and later returns from the why view\n\nIf `entire why` is invoked with no path in a non-interactive environment, it should return an error rather than attempting to print a directory listing.\n\nIf `--interactive` is supplied explicitly, the same rule should apply: failure is preferable to silently falling back to static output.\n\n## Data Flow\n\nThe command should follow this pipeline:\n\n1. Validate that the target path exists at `HEAD`\n2. If `--lines` / `-L` is present, parse numeric-only input:\n   - `N`\n   - `start-end`\n   Both `-L` and `--lines` should accept either form.\n3. Run `git blame --line-porcelain -- <path>` against `HEAD`\n   - when a line range is present, pass it through as `git blame -L <git-range>`\n3. Parse blame output into line records\n4. Collapse adjacent lines with the same blamed commit into contiguous blame blocks\n5. For each unique blamed commit:\n   - Resolve the local commit object\n   - Parse `Entire-Checkpoint` trailer if present\n   - If linked, read checkpoint summary and latest relevant session metadata from local checkpoint storage\n   - If the blamed commit object is missing locally, or checkpoint/session metadata is partially unavailable, degrade to plain blame metadata for the affected lines instead of aborting the whole overview\n6. Flatten blame blocks into a full-file row model that preserves the owning block for each source line\n7. Detect or derive the file language for syntax highlighting, preferably from the target filename and falling back safely when unknown\n8. Render syntax-highlighted code lines for the code pane in interactive mode\n9. Render either:\n   - a static text blame-like file view, or\n   - an interactive Bubble Tea model backed by the same row data\n10. In the no-arg interactive case, resolve the target path through a TUI file browser before loading blame data\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":4834},"formatted_output":"","status":"completed"}}
