enerated Checkpoint Summary`\n- `File Diff`\n\n`tab` cycles forward through those panes when more than one is present. The focused pane should:\n\n- receive the active bordered-panel treatment\n- own `j` / `k` or arrow-key scrolling when it is a scrollable pane\n- expand to show materially more content than the unfocused panes\n\nUnfocused panes may render as previews rather than full sections. In particular:\n\n- the session list may show only the first few rows plus a “more sessions” hint\n- the generated summary may show only an initial preview when unfocused\n- the diff may show only an initial preview when unfocused\n\n### Interactive Entry Without a Path\n\nIf `entire why` is invoked with no path in an interactive shell, it should start in a lightweight file browser rooted at the current working directory.\n\nThis browser should:\n\n- Show all files and directories, including dotfiles\n- Sort directories before files\n- Allow directory traversal with `j`/`k`, arrows, `enter`, and `h` or `backspace`\n- Preserve browser directory and cursor position when the user opens a file and later returns from the why view\n\nIf `entire why` is invoked with no path in a non-interactive environment, it should return an error rather than attempting to print a directory listing.\n\nIf `--interactive` is supplied explicitly, the same rule should apply: failure is preferable to silently falling back to static output.\n\n## Data Flow\n\nThe command should follow this pipeline:\n\n1. Validate that the target path exists at `HEAD`\n2. If `--lines` / `-L` is present, parse numeric-only input:\n   - `N`\n   - `start-end`\n   Both `-L` and `--lines` should accept either form.\n3. Run `git blame --line-porcelain -- <path>` against `HEAD`\n   - when a line range is present, pass it through as `git blame -L <git-range>`\n3. Parse blame output into line records\n4. Collapse adjacent lines with the same blamed commit into contiguous blame blocks\n5. For each unique blamed commit:\n   - Resolve the local commit object\n   - Parse `Entire-Checkpoint` trailer if present\n   - If linked, read checkpoint summary and latest relevant session metadata from local checkpoint storage\n   - If the blamed commit object is missing locally, or checkpoint/session metadata is partially unavailable, degrade to plain blame metadata for the affected lines instead of aborting the whole overview\n6. Flatten blame blocks into a full-file row model that preserves the owning block for each source line\n7. Detect or derive the file language for syntax highlighting, preferably from the target filename and falling back safely when unknown\n8. Render syntax-highlighted code lines for the code pane in interactive mode\n9. Render either:\n   - a static text blame-like file view, or\n   - an interactive Bubble Tea model backed by the same row data\n10. In the no-arg interactive case, resolve the target path through a TUI file browser before loading blame data\n"}}
{"timestamp":"2026-04-27T22:22:13.843Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"exec_command_end","call_id":"call_hVI42l3k67WNFHvINFq3AwXZ","process_id":"98061","turn_id":"019dd108-f70c-75e2-8c81-d9a95744ebd0","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '261,620p' docs/superpowers/specs/2026-04-21-entire-why-design.md"],"cwd":"/Users/pfleidi/entire/cli/.worktrees/feat/entire-why","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '261,620p' docs/superpowers/specs/2026-04-21-entire-why-design.md","name":"2026-04-21-entire-why-design.md","path":"docs/superpowers/specs/2026-04-21-entire-why-design.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","stdout":"","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"\nThe key invariants are:\n\n- Git determines which commit last touched the current line\n- Entire determines what extra context should be surfaced alongside that commit in the first view and in drill-down detail\n- Blame blocks remain the provenance and detail unit even though the UI is file-row-based\n- The first overview should degrade gracefully to plain blame behavior when optional Entire enrichment data is unavailable\n\n## Detail Views\n\n### Checkpoint Detail\n\nWhen a blamed commit links to an Entire checkpoint, the detail screen should show checkpoint-first information:\n\n- Checkpoint ID\n- Commit linkage\n- Agent\n- Summary if generated\n- Fallback summary when no generated summary exists\n- Files touched\n- Session count\n- Token usage when available\n- File-scoped diff for the selected file\n\nThe checkpoint detail screen should have clear visual separation between:\n\n- checkpoint metadata\n- the interactive session list\n- the file diff\n\nIt should then provide a path into session-level detail.\n\nFor multi-session checkpoints, the UI must not pretend there is always one obvious originating session. The checkpoint screen is the correct place to expose that ambiguity honestly.\n\nThe session list should be interactive, not a static blob of text. The user should be able to move between sessions and drill into one explicitly.\n\n### Session Detail\n\nSession detail is a drill-down from checkpoint detail.\n\nFor the prototype, this view should reuse or closely mirror current `entire explain --checkpoint` rendering rather than inventing a completely new transcript presentation layer.\n\nConcretely:\n\n- Transcript content should be rendered in the same condensed, human-readable style as `entire explain --checkpoint`, not as raw transcript JSON\n- Tool-call-heavy transcript stretches should be collapsed by default in the interactive session view\n- The user should be able to expand or collapse those tool-call groups on demand\n- Tool-call groups should be rendered as separate bordered sections, not only as inline text\n- `tab` should move forward between tool-call sections\n- `shift+tab` should move backward between tool-call sections\n- `e` should expand or collapse only the currently selected tool-call section\n- When a tool-call section gains focus through tabbing, the viewport should scroll to keep that section visible\n\nThis keeps the session view legible enough for demos while still preserving access to the underlying transcript flow.\n\n### Git Commit Fallback Detail\n\nWhen a blamed commit has no Entire linkage, the detail screen should show:\n\n- Commit hash\n- Author\n- Date\n- Commit subject\n- File-scoped diff for the selected file in that commit\n\nThis keeps the command valuable across mixed histories where some commits came from Entire sessions and others did not.\n\nFor the diff presentation, simple whole-diff syntax highlighting is not sufficient. The more useful rendering direction is:\n\n- preserve diff structure and hunk headers\n- show explicit green/red change indicators for added and removed lines\n- syntax-highlight the code portion of changed lines where practical\n\n## Reuse Opportunities\n\nThe prototype should reuse existing code where possible:\n\n- Commit-to-checkpoint resolution and trailer parsing from `entire explain`\n- Checkpoint summary and session readers from the `checkpoint` package\n- Pager behavior from `entire explain`\n- Bubble Tea browse/detail patterns from `search_tui.go`\n\nThe new command should avoid introducing a parallel metadata resolution stack when the `explain` path already has the local building blocks needed for prototype scope.\n\n## Risks\n\n### Provenance Join Ambiguity\n\nThe hardest part is not rendering but joining:\n\n`blame commit -> Entire-Checkpoint trailer -> checkpoint -> session detail`\n\nThis join is straightforward when one blamed commit has one checkpoint and one relevant session. It becomes ambiguous when a checkpoint contains multiple sessions.\n\nThe design handles this by making checkpoint detail the first drill-down level instead of jumping directly to a session.\n\n### Overreaching On Highlighting\n\nSyntax highlighting adds complexity in wrapping, width calculation, viewport scrolling, and theme selection. The prototype still accepts that cost because highlighted code materially improves the demo and better matches the reference experience.\n\nTo keep the scope controlled:\n\n- Use an existing pure-Go highlighting library rather than building a custom lexer path\n- Restrict built-in highlighting to the TUI code pane\n- Continue relying on pager-based highlighting for non-interactive output\n- Fall back gracefully to unhighlighted text if no lexer can be identified\n\nThere are now two distinct highlighting problems:\n\n- source-file highlighting in the main interactive file view\n- diff-aware highlighting in detail views\n\nThe second is more complex because useful diff presentation requires preserving add/remove semantics in addition to code coloring.\n\n### Performance On Large Files\n\nBlame parsing on very large files may be slow. The prototype accepts raw local blame latency rather than adding indexing or progressive loading upfront.\n\nIf performance is poor in practice, that should be addressed in a later iteration based on observed bottlenecks.\n\n## Deferred Questions\n\nThe following questions are intentionally deferred rather than solved in the prototype:\n\n- Whether the static first view should more closely mimic exact `git blame` formatting or keep a slightly more explicit Entire-oriented gutter\n- Whether Git fallback detail should show the full commit patch or only the selected file diff\n- Whether future `--json` output should model lines, blocks, or commit-grouped blocks\n- Whether later versions should support revision arguments, rename/copy tracking, or remote metadata fetching\n- Whether the no-arg file browser should later hide dotfiles by default or support filtering/toggling\n- Whether interactive file opening should support searching/filtering within the file browser\n- Whether a future `--browse` flag should exist separately from `--interactive`, or whether the current no-arg browser entry is sufficient\n\n## Recommended Implementation Phasing\n\nPhase 1:\n\n- Add `entire why <path>` in non-interactive mode\n- Parse local blame output into contiguous blocks\n- Enrich blocks with local Entire metadata when available\n- Add checkpoint detail and Git fallback detail\n- Reuse pager behavior\n\nPhase 2:\n\n- Add interactive TUI on top of the same display model\n- Rework the first screen so the file itself is the primary browsing surface\n- Add syntax-highlighted code rendering in the TUI code pane, preferably using Chroma-backed ANSI output\n- Add checkpoint detail, session list drill-down, and session transcript rendering modeled after `entire explain --checkpoint`\n- Add general pager navigation shortcuts and default tool-call collapsing in session detail\n\nPhase 3:\n\n- Add no-arg interactive entry through a file browser rooted at `pwd`\n- Preserve browser state when returning from a file\n- Polish visual layout, colors, and diff presentation based on demo feedback\n\nAcross those phases, `entire why` should preserve its richer multi-pane interaction model while converging toward the same documented control vocabulary and general TUI conventions used by other interactive Entire commands.\n\nThis sequencing reflects what the prototype exploration actually taught: the file-primary interactive view and the checkpoint/session drill-down are the core of the feature, and the browser entrypoint is a useful but secondary layer on top.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":2250},"formatted_output":"","status":"completed"}}
