{"timestamp":"2026-04-28T16:53:32.962Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"exec_command_end","call_id":"call_uvh1kP771p9GZB5vmUcGEIeA","process_id":"28344","turn_id":"019dd502-7bad-79b2-9f90-dba1174eeb64","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '1,260p' docs/superpowers/specs/2026-04-21-entire-why-design.md"],"cwd":"/Users/pfleidi/entire/cli/.worktrees/feat/entire-why","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,260p' docs/superpowers/specs/2026-04-21-entire-why-design.md","name":"2026-04-21-entire-why-design.md","path":"docs/superpowers/specs/2026-04-21-entire-why-design.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","stdout":"","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"# `entire why` Design\n\n## Goal\n\nIntroduce a prototype command named `entire why` that helps users understand why the current `HEAD` version of a file looks the way it does.\n\nThe command should feel like a blame-oriented history browser:\n\n- First view: a blame-derived full-file view of the current file\n- Second view: checkpoint detail for the selected blamed line's owning blame block when Entire metadata exists\n- Third view: session detail within that checkpoint\n- Optional no-arg interactive entry: a file browser rooted at the current working directory that lets the user choose a file before entering the why view\n\nFor commits that are not linked to Entire metadata, the command should still be useful by falling back to generic Git commit detail and a file-scoped diff.\n\n## Prototype Scope\n\nThe prototype is intentionally narrow.\n\nIncluded:\n\n- Command name: `entire why`\n- Input shape:\n  - `entire why <path>`\n  - `entire why -L <line> <path>`\n  - `entire why --lines <start-end> <path>`\n  - `entire why` in an interactive shell should open a file browser rooted at `pwd`\n- Explicit mode controls:\n  - `-i` / `--interactive` to force the TUI path\n  - `--no-pager` to disable pager use for static output\n- Local-only metadata resolution\n- `HEAD`-only blame view\n- Commit-first provenance model\n- Non-interactive text output\n- Interactive TUI when a real interactive shell is available\n- Checkpoint-first detail navigation\n- Summary text with fallback when no generated summary exists\n- Syntax highlighting in the TUI code pane\n\nExplicitly out of scope for the prototype:\n\n- Remote checkpoint fetching\n- Arbitrary revisions such as `entire why <rev> -- <path>`\n- Automatic summary generation during `entire why`\n- JSON output\n- Rename/copy-following flags such as `-M` and `-C`\n- Uncommitted working tree attribution\n\n## Product Decisions\n\n### Provenance Model\n\nThe first screen is commit-first, not checkpoint-first.\n\n`git blame` remains the source of truth for line provenance. Entire metadata enriches blamed commits by default when available, and that enrichment is part of the main value of `entire why`, not a secondary or hidden detail.\n\nThis is important because existing checkpoint/session storage is not sufficient to reconstruct exact current-line ownership without effectively reimplementing blame semantics.\n\n### History Coverage\n\nThe first screen should show the file itself in order from top to bottom, not a reduced list of blocks.\n\nEvery source line in the current `HEAD` version of the file should remain visible in both static and interactive modes. Entire metadata should enrich those lines, not replace the file view with a block browser.\n\nRows whose owning blame block has Entire metadata should be visually emphasized. Rows without Entire metadata should remain visible with blank or placeholder Entire fields.\n\n### Detail Hierarchy\n\nPressing `enter` or space on a selected blamed line should open a checkpoint-first detail screen when that line's owning blamed block has an `Entire-Checkpoint` trailer.\n\nIf no checkpoint exists for the blamed commit, detail falls back to generic Git commit information plus a file-scoped diff for the selected file.\n\n### Summary Policy\n\nThe first screen should show:\n\n- Saved generated summary text when present\n- Otherwise a fallback summary derived from existing local metadata, such as checkpoint intent or commit subject\n\n`entire why` should not generate summaries on demand in the prototype.\n\n## User Experience\n\n### First View\n\nThe first view should resemble an Entire-enriched blame browser rather than a plain blame dump or a generic table.\n\nConceptually it has one primary surface:\n\n- A full-file code view with a metadata gutter beside each source line\n\nThe file itself is the thing being browsed. Blame blocks remain important, but only as the provenance unit behind each line and as the target for detail drill-down.\n\nThe first view must do more than restate normal Git blame information. When Entire metadata is available for the blamed commit, that Entire context should be visible in the main screen by default so users can immediately understand not just who last touched the code, but also whether the change came through an Entire checkpoint and which agent or session context was involved.\n\nThe intended layout is:\n\n- Left side or metadata gutter: blame-oriented identity fields such as short commit hash, author, checkpoint ID, agent, and line number\n- Right side or code pane: the source line itself\n\nIn other words, the Entire-specific fields belong in the default scanning surface of the first screen, not only in an expanded detail view.\n\nEach visible file row should expose:\n\n- File line number\n- Short commit hash\n- Author\n- Checkpoint ID, when present\n- Agent name, when present\n- The source line itself\n\nFor Entire-linked lines, the main screen should aim to surface these fields directly in the primary browsing experience:\n\n- Entire checkpoint ID\n- AI agent used\n- Short AI-generated summary, when already a…2433 chars truncated…t controls:\n\n- arrow keys\n- `enter`\n- `esc`\n\nVim-style aliases should still exist, but they do not need to be shown in help text. The intended hidden alias policy is:\n\n- `h` = back\n- `j` = down\n- `k` = up\n- `l` = open / drill down / confirm\n\nThis means the documented controls remain accessibility-first while users familiar with vim-like navigation can still rely on the same directional vocabulary across views.\n\nThe TUI should include syntax highlighting in the code pane for the prototype.\n\nThe recommended approach is to use a pure-Go highlighter that emits ANSI-colored terminal text for direct rendering inside Bubble Tea viewports. The leading candidate is Chroma because it is pure Go, supports many languages, and can format to terminal color output. Markdown-focused renderers such as Glamour are less suitable as the primary engine because this screen is fundamentally a source-code browser, not a markdown document renderer.\n\nSyntax highlighting should be applied only to the code pane, not to the metadata gutter. The metadata side should remain styled via Lip Gloss so the two layers remain visually distinct.\n\nIn the interactive view, the selected line may also drive a compact metadata header showing richer fields such as date and summary, while the scrollable body stays focused on the file itself.\n\nThe interactive view should favor code readability over stuffing every Entire field into the per-line gutter. The settled layout direction is:\n\n- A compact left gutter for the file view with commit hash, author, checkpoint ID, and line number\n- A richer top metadata bar for the currently selected line, using labeled fields such as commit, author, date, checkpoint, agent, and summary\n- Detail screens that use clearly separated sections instead of one continuous text wall\n\nGeneral pager-style shortcuts should be supported in all TUI views:\n\n- `g` jumps to the top\n- `G` jumps to the bottom\n\nCheckpoint detail should use explicit focusable panes rather than treating the entire screen as one undifferentiated scroll surface. The current pane model is:\n\n- `Sessions`\n- `Generated Checkpoint Summary`\n- `File Diff`\n\n`tab` cycles forward through those panes when more than one is present. The focused pane should:\n\n- receive the active bordered-panel treatment\n- own `j` / `k` or arrow-key scrolling when it is a scrollable pane\n- expand to show materially more content than the unfocused panes\n\nUnfocused panes may render as previews rather than full sections. In particular:\n\n- the session list may show only the first few rows plus a “more sessions” hint\n- the generated summary may show only an initial preview when unfocused\n- the diff may show only an initial preview when unfocused\n\n### Interactive Entry Without a Path\n\nIf `entire why` is invoked with no path in an interactive shell, it should start in a lightweight file browser rooted at the current working directory.\n\nThis browser should:\n\n- Show all files and directories, including dotfiles\n- Sort directories before files\n- Allow directory traversal with `j`/`k`, arrows, `enter`, and `h` or `backspace`\n- Preserve browser directory and cursor position when the user opens a file and later returns from the why view\n\nIf `entire why` is invoked with no path in a non-interactive environment, it should return an error rather than attempting to print a directory listing.\n\nIf `--interactive` is supplied explicitly, the same rule should apply: failure is preferable to silently falling back to static output.\n\n## Data Flow\n\nThe command should follow this pipeline:\n\n1. Validate that the target path exists at `HEAD`\n2. If `--lines` / `-L` is present, parse numeric-only input:\n   - `N`\n   - `start-end`\n   Both `-L` and `--lines` should accept either form.\n3. Run `git blame --line-porcelain -- <path>` against `HEAD`\n   - when a line range is present, pass it through as `git blame -L <git-range>`\n3. Parse blame output into line records\n4. Collapse adjacent lines with the same blamed commit into contiguous blame blocks\n5. For each unique blamed commit:\n   - Resolve the local commit object\n   - Parse `Entire-Checkpoint` trailer if present\n   - If linked, read checkpoint summary and latest relevant session metadata from local checkpoint storage\n   - If the blamed commit object is missing locally, or checkpoint/session metadata is partially unavailable, degrade to plain blame metadata for the affected lines instead of aborting the whole overview\n6. Flatten blame blocks into a full-file row model that preserves the owning block for each source line\n7. Detect or derive the file language for syntax highlighting, preferably from the target filename and falling back safely when unknown\n8. Render syntax-highlighted code lines for the code pane in interactive mode\n9. Render either:\n   - a static text blame-like file view, or\n   - an interactive Bubble Tea model backed by the same row data\n10. In the no-arg interactive case, resolve the target path through a TUI file browser before loading blame data\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":2291},"formatted_output":"","status":"completed"}}
