mmitted working tree attribution\n53:The first screen is commit-first, not checkpoint-first.\n55:`git blame` remains the source of truth for line provenance. Entire metadata enriches blamed commits by default when available, and that enrichment is part of the main value of `entire why`, not a secondary or hidden detail.\n57:This is important because existing checkpoint/session storage is not sufficient to reconstruct exact current-line ownership without effectively reimplementing blame semantics.\n59:### History Coverage\n61:The first screen should show the file itself in order from top to bottom, not a reduced list of blocks.\n63:Every source line in the current `HEAD` version of the file should remain visible in both static and interactive modes. Entire metadata should enrich those lines, not replace the file view with a block browser.\n65:Rows whose owning blame block has Entire metadata should be visually emphasized. Rows without Entire metadata should remain visible with blank or placeholder Entire fields.\n69:Pressing `enter` or space on a selected blamed line should open a checkpoint-first detail screen when that line's owning blamed block has an `Entire-Checkpoint` trailer.\n71:If no checkpoint exists for the blamed commit, detail falls back to generic Git commit information plus a file-scoped diff for the selected file.\n77:- Saved generated summary text when present\n78:- Otherwise a fallback summary derived from existing local metadata, such as checkpoint intent or commit subject\n80:`entire why` should not generate summaries on demand in the prototype.\n86:The first view should resemble an Entire-enriched blame browser rather than a plain blame dump or a generic table.\n90:- A full-file code view with a metadata gutter beside each source line\n92:The file itself is the thing being browsed. Blame blocks remain important, but only as the provenance unit behind each line and as the target for detail drill-down.\n94:The first view must do more than restate normal Git blame information. When Entire metadata is available for the blamed commit, that Entire context should be visible in the main screen by default so users can immediately understand not just who last touched the code, but also whether the change came through an Entire checkpoint and which agent or session context was involved.\n98:- Left side or metadata gutter: blame-oriented identity fields such as short commit hash, author, checkpoint ID, agent, and line number\n99:- Right side or code pane: the source line itself\n101:In other words, the Entire-specific fields belong in the default scanning surface of the first screen, not only in an expanded detail view.\n108:- Checkpoint ID, when present\n109:- Agent name, when present\n112:For Entire-linked lines, the main screen should aim to surface these fields directly in the primary browsing experience:\n114:- Entire checkpoint ID\n115:- AI agent used\n116:- Short AI-generated summary, when already available\n117:- Fallback summary text when no generated summary exists\n119:The screen should therefore feel like \"blame plus Entire provenance\" rather than \"blame with a separate details screen.\"\n121:For non-Entire commits, those same metadata slots may be blank, replaced with placeholders, or omitted in a visually consistent way, but the layout should still preserve the same overall scanning model.\n123:Because summary text is often too wide to show next to every line without destroying file readability, the interactive view may surface summary in selected-line metadata rather than in every row of the gutter.\n125:### Non-Interactive Mode\n131:- Produce a readable text rendering of the file line-by-line\n132:- Work when stdout is piped\n133:- Work in accessibility mode\n134:- Preserve compatibility with pager-based presentation such as `less` or `de…7567 chars truncated…tire linkage, the detail screen should show:\n323:- Commit subject\n326:This keeps the command valuable across mixed histories where some commits came from Entire sessions and others did not.\n328:For the diff presentation, simple whole-diff syntax highlighting is not sufficient. The more useful rendering direction is:\n330:- preserve diff structure and hunk headers\n331:- show explicit green/red change indicators for added and removed lines\n332:- syntax-highlight the code portion of changed lines where practical\n336:The prototype should reuse existing code where possible:\n338:- Commit-to-checkpoint resolution and trailer parsing from `entire explain`\n339:- Checkpoint summary and session readers from the `checkpoint` package\n340:- Pager behavior from `entire explain`\n341:- Bubble Tea browse/detail patterns from `search_tui.go`\n343:The new command should avoid introducing a parallel metadata resolution stack when the `explain` path already has the local building blocks needed for prototype scope.\n345:## Risks\n347:### Provenance Join Ambiguity\n349:The hardest part is not rendering but joining:\n351:`blame commit -> Entire-Checkpoint trailer -> checkpoint -> session detail`\n353:This join is straightforward when one blamed commit has one checkpoint and one relevant session. It becomes ambiguous when a checkpoint contains multiple sessions.\n355:The design handles this by making checkpoint detail the first drill-down level instead of jumping directly to a session.\n357:### Overreaching On Highlighting\n359:Syntax highlighting adds complexity in wrapping, width calculation, viewport scrolling, and theme selection. The prototype still accepts that cost because highlighted code materially improves the demo and better matches the reference experience.\n361:To keep the scope controlled:\n363:- Use an existing pure-Go highlighting library rather than building a custom lexer path\n364:- Restrict built-in highlighting to the TUI code pane\n365:- Continue relying on pager-based highlighting for non-interactive output\n366:- Fall back gracefully to unhighlighted text if no lexer can be identified\n368:There are now two distinct highlighting problems:\n370:- source-file highlighting in the main interactive file view\n371:- diff-aware highlighting in detail views\n373:The second is more complex because useful diff presentation requires preserving add/remove semantics in addition to code coloring.\n375:### Performance On Large Files\n377:Blame parsing on very large files may be slow. The prototype accepts raw local blame latency rather than adding indexing or progressive loading upfront.\n379:If performance is poor in practice, that should be addressed in a later iteration based on observed bottlenecks.\n383:The following questions are intentionally deferred rather than solved in the prototype:\n385:- Whether the static first view should more closely mimic exact `git blame` formatting or keep a slightly more explicit Entire-oriented gutter\n386:- Whether Git fallback detail should show the full commit patch or only the selected file diff\n387:- Whether future `--json` output should model lines, blocks, or commit-grouped blocks\n388:- Whether later versions should support revision arguments, rename/copy tracking, or remote metadata fetching\n389:- Whether the no-arg file browser should later hide dotfiles by default or support filtering/toggling\n390:- Whether interactive file opening should support searching/filtering within the file browser\n391:- Whether a future `--browse` flag should exist separately from `--interactive`, or whether the current no-arg browser entry is sufficient\n393:## Recommended Implementation Phasing\n398:- Parse local blame output into contiguous blocks\n399:- Enrich blocks with local Entire metadata when available\n400:- Add checkpoint detail and Git fallback detail\n401:- Reuse pager behavior\n405:- Add interactive TUI on top of the same display model\n406:- Rework the first screen so the file itself is the primary browsing surface\n407:- Add syntax-highlighted code rendering in the TUI code pane, preferably using Chroma-backed ANSI output\n408:- Add checkpoint detail, session list drill-down, and session transcript rendering modeled after `entire explain --checkpoint`\n409:- Add general pager navigation shortcuts and default tool-call collapsing in session detail\n413:- Add no-arg interactive entry through a file browser rooted at `pwd`\n414:- Preserve browser state when returning from a file\n415:- Polish visual layout, colors, and diff presentation based on demo feedback\n417:Across those phases, `entire why` should preserve its richer multi-pane interaction model while converging toward the same documented control vocabulary and general TUI conventions used by other interactive Entire commands.\n419:This sequencing reflects what the prototype exploration actually taught: the file-primary interactive view and the checkpoint/session drill-down are the core of the feature, and the browser entrypoint is a useful but secondary layer on top.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":2500},"formatted_output":"","status":"completed"}}
