{"timestamp":"2026-04-28T16:54:20.692Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"exec_command_end","call_id":"call_ZNNSbpbQICkrQdbgU1ULzrFx","process_id":"49681","turn_id":"019dd502-7bad-79b2-9f90-dba1174eeb64","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '60,220p' docs/superpowers/specs/2026-04-21-entire-why-design.md"],"cwd":"/Users/pfleidi/entire/cli/.worktrees/feat/entire-why","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '60,220p' docs/superpowers/specs/2026-04-21-entire-why-design.md","name":"2026-04-21-entire-why-design.md","path":"docs/superpowers/specs/2026-04-21-entire-why-design.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","stdout":"","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"\nThe first screen should show the file itself in order from top to bottom, not a reduced list of blocks.\n\nEvery source line in the current `HEAD` version of the file should remain visible in both static and interactive modes. Entire metadata should enrich those lines, not replace the file view with a block browser.\n\nRows whose owning blame block has Entire metadata should be visually emphasized. Rows without Entire metadata should remain visible with blank or placeholder Entire fields.\n\n### Detail Hierarchy\n\nPressing `enter` on a selected blamed line should open a checkpoint-first detail screen when that line's owning blamed block has an `Entire-Checkpoint` trailer.\n\nIf no checkpoint exists for the blamed commit, detail falls back to generic Git commit information plus a file-scoped diff for the selected file.\n\n### Summary Policy\n\nThe first screen should show:\n\n- Saved generated summary text when present\n- Otherwise a fallback summary derived from existing local metadata, such as checkpoint intent or commit subject\n\n`entire why` should not generate summaries on demand in the prototype.\n\n## User Experience\n\n### First View\n\nThe first view should resemble an Entire-enriched blame browser rather than a plain blame dump or a generic table.\n\nConceptually it has one primary surface:\n\n- A full-file code view with a metadata gutter beside each source line\n\nThe file itself is the thing being browsed. Blame blocks remain important, but only as the provenance unit behind each line and as the target for detail drill-down.\n\nThe first view must do more than restate normal Git blame information. When Entire metadata is available for the blamed commit, that Entire context should be visible in the main screen by default so users can immediately understand not just who last touched the code, but also whether the change came through an Entire checkpoint and which agent or session context was involved.\n\nThe intended layout is:\n\n- Left side or metadata gutter: blame-oriented identity fields such as short commit hash, author, checkpoint ID, agent, and line number\n- Right side or code pane: the source line itself\n\nIn other words, the Entire-specific fields belong in the default scanning surface of the first screen, not only in an expanded detail view.\n\nEach visible file row should expose:\n\n- File line number\n- Short commit hash\n- Author\n- Checkpoint ID, when present\n- Agent name, when present\n- The source line itself\n\nFor Entire-linked lines, the main screen should aim to surface these fields directly in the primary browsing experience:\n\n- Entire checkpoint ID\n- AI agent used\n- Short AI-generated summary, when already available\n- Fallback summary text when no generated summary exists\n\nThe screen should therefore feel like \"blame plus Entire provenance\" rather than \"blame with a separate details screen.\"\n\nFor non-Entire commits, those same metadata slots may be blank, replaced with placeholders, or omitted in a visually consistent way, but the layout should still preserve the same overall scanning model.\n\nBecause summary text is often too wide to show next to every line without destroying file readability, the interactive view may surface summary in selected-line metadata rather than in every row of the gutter.\n\n### Non-Interactive Mode\n\nNon-interactive mode should be the baseline behavior.\n\nIt should:\n\n- Produce a readable text rendering of the file line-by-line\n- Work when stdout is piped\n- Work in accessibility mode\n- Preserve compatibility with pager-based presentation such as `less` or `delta`\n- Support line-restricted views via `--lines` / `-L`\n\nIf output is long, the command should use the same pager strategy already used by `entire explain`.\n\nFor non-interactive output, pager-based syntax highlighting remains acceptable and desirable. Users who already route CLI output through tools such as `delta` should be able to benefit from that setup.\n\nThe current command contract is intentionally split:\n\n- interactive terminal without overrides: default to the TUI\n- non-interactive, piped, or accessibility mode: default to static output\n- `--interactive`: force the TUI and fail clearly if a real interactive terminal is unavailable\n- `--no-pager`: keep static output but bypass pager integration\n\nIn implementation terms, the TUI path should require all of the following:\n\n- accessibility mode is not enabled\n- output is attached to a terminal-capable writer\n- interactive prompting is possible under the CLI's normal `CanPromptInteractively()` rules\n\n### Interactive Mode\n\nWhen a real interactive shell is available, the command should offer a TUI.\n\nThe TUI should reuse established Bubble Tea interaction patterns already used elsewhere in the CLI:\n\n- Optional file-browser mode when `entire why` is invoked without a path\n- Browse mode for the full file\n- Detail mode for checkpoint or commit detail\n- Session detail mode\n- Viewport scrolling for long content\n- shared `bubbles/key` bindings from the CLI key map rather than ad hoc string matching\n- arrow keys plus `j` and `k` for vertical movement\n- `home` / `end` plus `g` / `G` for top and bottom navigation\n- `enter` to select, open, or drill down\n- `esc` to go back to the previous nested view\n- `q` or `ctrl+c` to quit the TUI from any view\n\nThe visible help text should use the same vocabulary as current Entire TUIs and should stay aligned with the shared key map:\n\n- `q` = quit\n- `esc` = back or cancel\n- `/` = search, only in views that actually support search\n- `enter` = select/open/search depending on the current mode\n- `↑/↓, j/k` = scroll or move selection\n- `home/end, g/G` = top/bottom\n- `n/p` or `n/→` and `p/←` = next/previous page only in paged views\n\nHelp footers should be width-aware like the activity and search TUIs:\n\n- wide layouts may show the full compact aliases, such as `↑/↓, j/k scroll` and `home/end, g/G top/bottom`\n- narrower layouts should drop aliases before dropping the primary control\n- very narrow layouts may reduce the footer to the single most important action or just the scroll percentage\n- help text must not overflow or wrap awkwardly\n\nVim-style aliases should exist where they are part of the shared vocabulary, but `enter` remains the confirm/open key. Do not introduce a separate `l` confirm path unless the shared CLI key map grows that binding.\n\nWhen a view has nested navigation, `h` and `backspace` may be accepted as additional back aliases for consistency with existing search detail behavior, but visible help should continue to describe the shared `esc` back binding. At the root browse view, `esc` may exit because there is no parent view to return to; `q` always means quit.\n\nThe TUI should include syntax highlighting in the code pane for the prototype.\n\nThe recommended approach is to use a pure-Go highlighter that emits ANSI-colored terminal text for direct rendering inside Bubble Tea viewports. The leading candidate is Chroma because it is pure Go, supports many languages, and can format to terminal color output. Markdown-focused renderers such as Glamour are less suitable as the primary engine because this screen is fundamentally a source-code browser, not a markdown document renderer.\n\nSyntax highlighting should be applied only to the code pane, not to the metadata gutter. The metadata side should remain styled via Lip Gloss so the two layers remain visually distinct.\n\nIn the interactive view, the selected line may also drive a compact metadata header showing richer fields such as date and summary, while the scrollable body stays focused on the file itself.\n\nThe interactive view should favor code readability over stuffing every Entire field into the per-line gutter. The settled layout direction is:\n\n- A compact left gutter for the file view with commit hash, author, checkpoint ID, and line number\n- A richer top metadata bar for the currently selected line, using labeled fields such as commit, author, date, checkpoint, agent, and summary\n- Detail screens that use clearly separated sections instead of one continuous text wall\n\nGeneral pager-style shortcuts should be supported in all TUI views:\n\n- `home` or `g` jumps to the top\n- `end` or `G` jumps to the bottom\n\nCheckpoint detail should use explicit focusable panes rather than treating the entire screen as one undifferentiated scroll surface. The current pane model is:\n\n- `Sessions`\n- `Generated Checkpoint Summary`\n- `File Diff`\n\n`tab` cycles forward through those panes when more than one is present. The focused pane should:\n\n- receive the active bordered-panel treatment\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":3041},"formatted_output":"","status":"completed"}}
