{"timestamp":"2026-04-30T17:26:04.082Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"agent_message","message":"The prototype was fast because its per-key render pass was much cheaper. It did still call `SetContent()` on every `j/k`, but the content it rebuilt was simple.\n\nMain differences:\n\n- Prototype gutter was fixed-width and simple: `commit author checkpoint line`.\n  Current gutter is dynamic: `time author agents commit checkpoint line`, computes max metadata width, styles each field, and calls `lipgloss.Width`.\n\n- Current code has an accidental O(n²) path:\n  `renderGutter()` calls `whyLineColumnWidth(m.data.Rows)` twice per row, and `whyLineColumnWidth` scans all rows. On a 2,400-line file, that is millions of extra row scans per key press.\n\n- Syntax highlighting changed from one whole-buffer Chroma call in the prototype:\n  `quick.Highlight(..., \"terminal256\", \"dracula\")`\n  to current per-line formatting:\n  `Tokenise` once, then `formatters.TTY16m.Format` for every line.\n  That is much more allocation-heavy.\n\n- Current branch added more per-row work:\n  agent normalization/sorting, `timeAgo`, dynamic metadata padding, multiple `lipgloss.Style.Render` calls per visible blame block, and `viewport.SetContent()` still causes Bubble’s viewport to split and width-scan the whole content.\n\nThe rendering bugs in your screenshots are from my latest experimental change, not from the original lag: I bypassed `viewport.View()` and rendered visible rows manually. That avoids some work, but breaks Bubble Tea’s normal repaint/clearing behavior, so old footer/body rows remain on screen and ANSI-wrapped code corrupts the layout.\n\nSo the right fix is not the custom visible-row renderer. It should be reverted. Then the actual optimization should keep Bubble’s viewport rendering but make the content cheaper/stable: cache line width, cache highlighted code, remove the O(n²) line-width calls, and probably switch highlighting back closer to the prototype’s whole-buffer formatter or cache it on resize only.","phase":"final_answer","memory_citation":null}}
{"timestamp":"2026-04-30T17:26:04.082Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"The prototype was fast because its per-key render pass was much cheaper. It did still call `SetContent()` on every `j/k`, but the content it rebuilt was simple.\n\nMain differences:\n\n- Prototype gutter was fixed-width and simple: `commit author checkpoint line`.\n  Current gutter is dynamic: `time author agents commit checkpoint line`, computes max metadata width, styles each field, and calls `lipgloss.Width`.\n\n- Current code has an accidental O(n²) path:\n  `renderGutter()` calls `whyLineColumnWidth(m.data.Rows)` twice per row, and `whyLineColumnWidth` scans all rows. On a 2,400-line file, that is millions of extra row scans per key press.\n\n- Syntax highlighting changed from one whole-buffer Chroma call in the prototype:\n  `quick.Highlight(..., \"terminal256\", \"dracula\")`\n  to current per-line formatting:\n  `Tokenise` once, then `formatters.TTY16m.Format` for every line.\n  That is much more allocation-heavy.\n\n- Current branch added more per-row work:\n  agent normalization/sorting, `timeAgo`, dynamic metadata padding, multiple `lipgloss.Style.Render` calls per visible blame block, and `viewport.SetContent()` still causes Bubble’s viewport to split and width-scan the whole content.\n\nThe rendering bugs in your screenshots are from my latest experimental change, not from the original lag: I bypassed `viewport.View()` and rendered visible rows manually. That avoids some work, but breaks Bubble Tea’s normal repaint/clearing behavior, so old footer/body rows remain on screen and ANSI-wrapped code corrupts the layout.\n\nSo the right fix is not the custom visible-row renderer. It should be reverted. Then the actual optimization should keep Bubble’s viewport rendering but make the content cheaper/stable: cache line width, cache highlighted code, remove the O(n²) line-width calls, and probably switch highlighting back closer to the prototype’s whole-buffer formatter or cache it on resize only."}],"phase":"final_answer"}}
